36:["$","details","vocab-p2",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",2]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"In paragraph 1, the word load refers to —"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"B. the total demand for electricity the system must supply"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Load is a multi-meaning word. Here it is defined by what follows: air conditioners, ovens, and commuters all drawing power at once. That is electrical demand."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"a quantity of goods being transported"}]," ","— ","A common meaning of load, but nothing in this passage is being transported."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"the total demand for electricity the system must supply"}]," ","— ","Correct. Paragraph 1 spells out what makes up the load — simultaneous electricity use."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"a burden of responsibility carried by utility workers"}]," ","— ","A figurative meaning the passage does not use. The strain described is on the grid, not on workers."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"the weight of equipment on transmission towers"}]," ","— ","Physical weight is a real meaning of load but is unrelated to peak demand hours."]}]]}]]]}]]}]
37:["$","details","vocab-p3",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",3]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"In paragraph 4, the phrase walking a tightrope in the wind is an example of which type of figurative language, and what does it mean?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"C. Metaphor; managing the grid in August requires fine balance under conditions that could upset it at any moment."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"The comparison is stated directly, with no like or as, which makes it a metaphor. The final sentence interprets it for you: a thin margin, with immediate consequences if balance is lost."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Personification; the grid is described as if it were a person."}]," ","— ","No human qualities are given to the grid; the image describes the operators' task."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Hyperbole; the author exaggerates to argue that the grid will fail."}]," ","— ","The author is not exaggerating — the last sentence treats the risk as real and specific."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Metaphor; managing the grid in August requires fine balance under conditions that could upset it at any moment."}]," ","— ","Correct. A direct comparison, and the passage explains exactly what it stands for."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Idiom; it is a fixed expression meaning to work slowly and carefully."}]," ","— ","It is a vivid comparison built for this passage, not a fixed everyday expression."]}]]}]]]}]]}]
38:["$","details","vocab-p4",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",4]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"Which context clue best explains the meaning of brownout in paragraph 3?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"C. A definition clue explaining that voltage is lowered slightly and once made bulbs visibly dim"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"The paragraph gives the cause (voltage lowered slightly) and the historical effect (incandescent bulbs dimming) in the same breath. That is a definition clue."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"A contrast clue comparing it to a blackout"}]," ","— ","Blackout never appears in the passage."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"An example clue listing appliances that stop working"}]," ","— ","No appliances are listed in paragraph 3, and nothing stops working."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"A definition clue explaining that voltage is lowered slightly and once made bulbs visibly dim"}]," ","— ","Correct. The meaning is stated directly around the word."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"An inference clue based on the word brown"}]," ","— ","Guessing from the color would not produce the meaning. The passage supplies it instead."]}]]}]]]}]]}]
39:["$","section","vocab-challenge",{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-display text-lg font-bold text-eng-navy-900","children":"Challenge"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","details","vocab-c1",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",1]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"As used in paragraph 2, the word nonsettleable most nearly means —"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"D. unable to sink out of the water on its own"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"The sentence gives you almost nothing, so take the word apart. Non- means not, -able means able to, and settle, as paragraph 3 uses it, means to sink to the bottom. Put together, nonsettleable means not able to sink out on its own. Then confirm with context: in paragraph 3 the floc \"does what the fine clay could not: it settles.\""]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"quick to sink to the bottom"}]," ","— ","This ignores the prefix. Non- means not, so the word describes particles that do not sink — which is why operators have to add a coagulant at all."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"impossible to agree on or resolve"}]," ","— ","This builds the word from the most common meaning of settle, as in settling an argument. Paragraph 3 shows the meaning used here: the floc \"settles, sinking to the floor of the basin.\""]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"too small to be seen"}]," ","— ","Size is the cause of the trouble, not the meaning of the word. None of its parts — non-, settle, -able — means small or unseen."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"unable to sink out of the water on its own"}]," ","— ","Correct. Non- (not) + settle (sink, as paragraph 3 uses it) + -able (able to) = not able to sink. Paragraph 3 confirms it: the floc \"does what the fine clay could not.\""]}]]}]]]}]]}],["$","details","vocab-c2",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":["$L3f","$L40"]}],"$L41","$L42"]}]]}]
3a:["$","$L2f","vocab-m2",{"question":{"id":"vocab-m2","type":"vocabulary-in-context","question":"As used in the passage, a pretax deduction is one that is —","passageId":"vocab-mastery-paystub","choices":["taken out before income tax is figured","taken out after income tax has been figured","paid by the employer instead of the worker","a tax paid ahead of time for next year"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Pre- means before, the same prefix as in preapproval. A pretax deduction comes out before income tax is figured, which is why, as the passage says, it \"lowers the amount of pay that income tax is figured on.\" If you missed this, revisit \"Word parts get you close enough\" and find pre- in the table.","choiceRationales":["Correct. Pre- means before, and the rest of the sentence confirms it: taking the money out first \"lowers the amount of pay that income tax is figured on.\"","This reverses the prefix. Pre- means before, not after — and a deduction taken after tax is figured could not lower the amount that is taxed.","Fits the sentence grammatically, but the passage describes deductions as amounts \"taken out\" of your pay. Nothing says the employer pays them.","Pre- does mean before, but a pretax deduction is not a tax at all. It is a deduction that comes out before tax is figured."],"skill":"vocabulary","difficulty":"Developing"},"number":2}]
3b:["$","$L2f","vocab-m3",{"question":{"id":"vocab-m3","type":"vocabulary-in-context","question":"As used in the passage, the word gross most nearly means —","passageId":"vocab-mastery-paystub","choices":["disgusting or unpleasant","total, before anything is subtracted","left over after taxes and deductions","larger than it should be"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Gross has several meanings, and the everyday one, disgusting, is the one this item is built to catch. The next sentence decides it: gross pay is the biggest number \"because nothing has been subtracted from it yet.\" Substitute total, before anything is subtracted, and the sentence still reads correctly. If you missed this, revisit \"Multi-meaning words are decided by the sentence.\"","choiceRationales":["The most common meaning of gross, but it makes no sense on a pay stub. Substitute it back into the sentence and the sentence falls apart.","Correct. Gross pay is the biggest number because \"nothing has been subtracted from it yet\" — it is the whole amount.","This describes net pay, the amount at the bottom of the stub. Gross pay is the starting figure, before anything comes out.","Gross can describe something excessive, but the passage gives a plain reason for the big number: nothing has been taken out yet. Nothing is wrong with it."],"skill":"vocabulary","difficulty":"TABE Ready"},"number":3}]
3c:["$","$L2f","vocab-m4",{"question":{"id":"vocab-m4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"The author says that a pay stub is \"a receipt for your time.\" What does the author mean?","passageId":"vocab-mastery-paystub","choices":["A pay stub is printed on the same thin paper as a store receipt.","Workers are paid according to the time of day they work.","Workers should keep every pay stub for at least a year.","A pay stub is the record of what you were paid for the hours you worked, so it deserves the same check you would give any receipt."],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"Calling a pay stub a receipt is a metaphor: it states the comparison directly. A receipt is a record you check against what you actually handed over. Here, what you handed over is your time, so the stub is the record of what you were paid for your hours — and the next sentence tells you to check it. If you missed this, revisit \"Figurative language says one thing to mean another\": the literal-minded choice restates the image instead of interpreting it.","choiceRationales":["This restates the image literally. The author is not describing paper; the comparison is about what a receipt is for.","This misreads the image. In \"a receipt for your time,\" time means the hours you gave to the job, not the time of day you worked them.","Sensible advice, but the passage never says how long to keep a stub. It says to check it \"before you put it away.\"","Correct. A receipt records an exchange so you can check it. Here the exchange is your time for your pay, and the next sentence tells you to check it."],"skill":"vocabulary","difficulty":"TABE Ready"},"number":4}]
3e:["$","$L2f","vocab-c4",{"question":{"id":"vocab-c4","type":"short-response","question":"The word turbid in paragraph 1 is never defined. In three or four sentences, explain what turbid means, name the kind of context clue that told you, quote the words that gave you the clue, and check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.","passageId":"vocab-challenge-water","explanation":"A complete answer does three jobs: it gives a meaning close to cloudy or muddy with fine particles, it names the clue type and quotes the words that supply it, and it confirms the meaning by substitution. Because the passage describes a situation instead of defining the word, the clue is an inference clue. The substitution step is the one most answers skip, and it is the one that catches a wrong guess such as rough or fast-moving, which comes from confusing turbid with turbulent.","sampleResponse":"In paragraph 1, turbid means cloudy or muddy, so full of fine particles that you cannot see through it. The passage never defines the word, so I used an inference clue: runoff has carried \"clay and silt\" into the river, and \"a coin dropped into a glass of that water could vanish from sight before it reached the bottom.\" The last sentence of the paragraph also says the water \"has to be made clear,\" which means it is not clear when it arrives. When I put my meaning back in, the river can \"arrive at the treatment plant\" cloudy with silt, and the sentence still makes sense.","skill":"vocabulary","difficulty":"Challenge"},"number":4}]
3f:["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",2]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"As used in paragraph 3, the word clarified most nearly means —"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}]
40:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"C. became clear as the solid matter sank out"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Clarify usually means to explain, and that is the meaning this item is built to catch. A contrast clue decides it here: the water \"is still murky, but\" by the far end of the basin it has clarified, so clarified must mean the opposite of murky. The same paragraph gives the reason — the floc has sunk to the floor. Substitute became clear and the sentence reads correctly; substitute was explained and it does not."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"explained in simpler terms"}]," ","— ","The most common meaning of clarify, and the trap. Nothing is being explained in paragraph 3. Put this meaning back into the sentence and it no longer makes sense."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"cooled down"}]," ","— ","Fits the sentence grammatically, but temperature never comes up. The contrast is with \"murky,\" so the change is in how the water looks."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"became clear as the solid matter sank out"}]," ","— ","Correct. The contrast clue sets clarified against \"murky,\" and the floc sinking to the floor of the basin explains how the water got that way."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"became completely safe to drink"}]," ","— ","Too strong. Paragraph 4 says clear water is still \"not finished\" — it has not yet been filtered or disinfected."]}]]}]]]}]
41:["$","details","vocab-c3",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",3]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"In paragraph 5, the author writes that treated water is \"a promise the plant has to keep all the way to the tap.\" What does the author most likely mean?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"B. Making water safe at the plant is not enough; the plant has to keep it safe through every mile of pipe until it reaches the faucet."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"This is a metaphor: the author states directly that treated water is a promise. A promise is not complete when it is made; it has to be kept over time. Read with the rest of paragraph 5, the image means the plant's job continues after the water leaves — which is exactly what the residual is for."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The plant signs a written guarantee promising each customer safe water."}]," ","— ","This restates the image literally. No written guarantee appears in the passage; the promise stands for a responsibility the plant carries all the way to the faucet."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Making water safe at the plant is not enough; the plant has to keep it safe through every mile of pipe until it reaches the faucet."}]," ","— ","Correct. It interprets the image instead of restating it, and it connects to the residual that \"guards against recontamination along the way.\""]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["C","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Most tap water becomes unsafe before it reaches the faucet."}]," ","— ","Too strong, and not in the passage. Paragraph 5 says contamination \"can enter\" old pipes, not that most water becomes unsafe. The residual exists to prevent that."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["D","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Tap water is a product the plant finishes and sells, like anything else."}]," ","— ","Contradicted by the same sentence, which says treated water is \"not a finished product.\""]}]]}]]]}]]}]
42:["$","details","vocab-c4",{"className":"group rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex cursor-pointer list-none items-start gap-3 rounded-xl p-4 font-medium text-eng-navy-900 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-eng-teal-600","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 rounded bg-eng-navy-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold text-eng-navy-700","children":["Q",4]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed","children":"The word turbid in paragraph 1 is never defined. In three or four sentences, explain what turbid means, name the kind of context clue that told you, quote the words that gave you the clue, and check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-teal-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-teal-800","children":["Model response:"," "]}],"In paragraph 1, turbid means cloudy or muddy, so full of fine particles that you cannot see through it. The passage never defines the word, so I used an inference clue: runoff has carried \"clay and silt\" into the river, and \"a coin dropped into a glass of that water could vanish from sight before it reached the bottom.\" The last sentence of the paragraph also says the water \"has to be made clear,\" which means it is not clear when it arrives. When I put my meaning back in, the river can \"arrive at the treatment plant\" cloudy with silt, and the sentence still makes sense."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"A complete answer does three jobs: it gives a meaning close to cloudy or muddy with fine particles, it names the clue type and quotes the words that supply it, and it confirms the meaning by substitution. Because the passage describes a situation instead of defining the word, the clue is an inference clue. The substitution step is the one most answers skip, and it is the one that catches a wrong guess such as rough or fast-moving, which comes from confusing turbid with turbulent."]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]
